    description: "[REVIEW 2026-11-01, MIS-PREMISED] 2026-11-01 review at dev 100b6fbb6e: the RAW tree-sitter-objc parse of AFHTTPSessionManager.h / AFURLSessionManager.h does lose every property_declaration (0/4, 0/11, has_error True), but the analyzer never uses the raw tree: _objc_parse_source retries through the WI-lafom rewrite (AF_API_* availability rule) and gets 4/4 and 11/11, has_error False. analyze_objc emits 15 property symbols today and also did with objc.py from 6ac135c868 (the commit at filing time). Default survey output omits them because noise_filter lists objc in _NOISE_PROPERTY_LANGUAGES (WI-tisom, 313eaf8622) - intentional; --include-docs shows them. (Original text below; the review entry in the thread has the evidence.)\n\nFound by WI-vufur's measurement. AFNetworking's AFHTTPSessionManager.h and AFURLSessionManager.h emit 0 of their 4 and 0 of their 11 @property declarations (methods are all emitted: 11/11, 26/26). tree-sitter-objc does not recognise the @interface block in those headers (likely the API macros: NS_ASSUME_NONNULL_BEGIN, AF_API_UNAVAILABLE / API_AVAILABLE attributes, nullable qualifiers -- NOT CHECKED which), and recovers each @property line as a top-level expression_statement, so the objc analyzer's property_declaration arm never sees one. No ERROR node surrounds the recovered lines, so WI-bulaz's partial-parse row for the file does not locate the loss.\n\nRepro: parse AFNetworking/AFHTTPSessionManager.h with get_language(\"objc\"); the @property lines sit under translation_unit > expression_statement. A minimal \"@interface A : NSObject @property (nonatomic, strong) NSString *s; @end\" parses to class_interface > property_declaration.\n\nTo do: bisect which construct breaks the @interface parse; measure how many ObjC headers in the corpus (AFNetworking, SDWebImage, CocoaLumberjack, ResearchKit, GPUImage) lose properties this way; then decide between pre-processing the known macros and recovering properties from expression_statement shapes."  # 6f50
